      "question": {
        ":question/id": "c8932d66-35b8-594b-9a87-80583a008ed2",
        ":question/problem": "The decay rate $M$ of muons in a beam traveling at high speed can be modeled by the function $M(t)=2\\cdot {e}^{-2t},$ where $t$ is the time, in microseconds, since observations started. What will be the decay rate of the muons after $0.5$ microseconds? Round your answer to three decimal places.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The function $M(t)$ models the decay rate for the particles $t$ microseconds since observations started.\n\nThus, the decay rate of the muons after $0.5$ microseconds is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(0.5) & =2\\cdot {e}^{-2(0.5)} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{-1} \\\\  & \u22480.736.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the decay rate of the particles is $0.736$ muons per microsecond.",
